\f1\fs22 \
\'93We are the lucky few who have been granted the gift of life and we must use this precious gift to effect positive change. Who are we to complain if we do nothing to improve our situation? Who are we to complain about the inevitability of death? What of all the humans that could have been? The one\'92s that were never born? Most have Never Stirred.\'94\
the High Elvis, strummed his 12 string and effortlessly slipped into a heart-felt ballad for those who have never been.\
\
While Pastor Rex Humble's gospel fervour spread through the Ghetto like wildfire, the Evolution Elvi hatched a plan so audacious it bordered on heresy\'97not religious heresy, but the far more dangerous social kind. They would hold a concert in The Domain, the pristine heart of Airborne territory, where Cloudborne rarely set foot except to clean or serve.\
The logistics were nightmarish. Permits were mysteriously approved despite bureaucratic resistance\'97rumors suggested Eloise Shakti's invisible hand at work. Security arrangements required delicate negotiations with both PAPER and Fridge Force. Most shocking was the venue itself: the sprawling green expanse of The Domain, normally reserved for Airborne charity galas and corporate coming-of-age ceremonies.\
As dawn broke on concert day, something unprecedented occurred. Cloudborne began arriving in droves, using public transportation routes normally off-limits to them. They came cautiously at first, expecting Demographic Culling at any moment. Instead, they found Airborne youth already gathered, wearing homemade jumpsuits over designer clothes in awkward solidarity.\
By midday, half a million people filled The Domain\'97Cloudborne, Airborne, and every variation between. Vendors from the Ghetto set up food stalls selling fried peanut butter sandwiches alongside delicate Airborne canap\'e9s. Children from both sides of the Layer played together, the Cloudborne teaching the Airborne how to properly style a pompadour with makeshift hair products.\
"Have you ever seen anything like this?" whispered a stunned Channel Forty-Eight reporter to her cameraman, who shook his head in equal disbelief. Their live broadcast showed Airborne executives sitting on blankets beside Cloudborne maintenance workers, all waiting for the Evolution Elvi to take the stage.\
The concert itself was a masterpiece of programming. It began with smaller Elvi bands playing science-themed rock songs, with titles like "Natural Selection Suite" and "The Descent of Man (Into Rock and Roll)." Between sets, notable Evolutionaries gave short talks on basic scientific principles, making complex ideas accessible through Elvis metaphors. "Gravity is like a good bass line," explained one particularly charismatic speaker. "It holds everything together, baby."\
As twilight approached, the atmosphere grew electric. Massive screens displayed images from the cosmos alongside pictures of Elvis Presley through the years\'97evolution of both stars and star compressed into a visual symphony. The crowd, initially segregated by habit, had thoroughly integrated, distinctions between Airborne and Cloudborne blurring with each passing hour.\
When the High Elvis finally emerged, the roar was deafening. He wore a jumpsuit unlike any seen before\'97half traditional white rhinestones, half styled with scientific formulas and DNA helixes executed in sequins. His band, composed of the finest musicians from both above and below the Layer, launched into a medley of classic Elvi hits reworked with subtle evolutionary themes.\
The concert reached its emotional peak during a quiet, reflective moment when the High Elvis addressed the crowd directly. His words about the lottery of existence resonated across the field as he spoke of the privilege of life itself. When he began strumming his 12-string guitar and sang "Most Have Never Stirred," the entire crowd fell silent. The ballad's poignant reminder of life's brevity and preciousness brought tears to eyes both Airborne and Cloudborne alike.\
As the final notes faded, something remarkable happened. Airborne executives removed their expensive jackets. Cloudborne workers stood taller. For perhaps the first time, they saw each other not as separate classes but as fellow lottery winners in the grand cosmic game of existence.\
the High Elvis allowed the moment to breathe before transitioning to the concert's final movement. With renewed energy, he launched into a blistering critique of the forces dividing society\'97both religious fundamentalism and the economic stratification that artificially separated humanity.\
"We can all be angels, that's the way that we'll evolve," he sang, and the crowd joined in, the melody simple enough to learn immediately, the message powerful enough to resonate deeply. "No longer Cloud- or Airborne, anyone may rise above!"\
By the time he reached the climactic anthem "Religious Minds," the audience was completely unified in purpose. Airborne and Cloudborne swayed together, arms linked, singing the refrain with passionate conviction. The video screens showed the contrast\'97images of Pastor Humble's segregated congregation alongside the integrated masses of The Domain.\
CEO Dunhilton, watching remotely from his penthouse, choked on his artisanal water. "This is... problematic," he muttered to Gerry Hardman, who maintained his usual inscrutable expression.\
"Indeed, sir," Gerry replied, watching as the cameras panned across what could only be described as the beginning of a revolution. "It appears the TMinus-Virus deployment timeline may need adjustment."\
Back at The Domain, as the final chords rang out and fireworks painted evolutionary spirals across the night sky, the High Elvis raised his hands. "Science tells us we are all stardust," his amplified voice proclaimed. "And if stardust can become Elvis, imagine what else it can become!"\
The crowd's response shook the ground beneath their feet, the vibrations traveling up through the Layer and beyond, rippling through a society that suddenly seemed less rigidly stratified than it had been just hours before.\
In the Ghetto, Pastor Rex Humble felt a tremor pass through the floor of New Graceland. He paused mid-sermon, a flicker of uncertainty crossing his face for perhaps the first time since his conversion.\
"Trouble," he whispered to himself, reaching for his gospel-emblazoned microphone with renewed determination. "The devil works through bass lines and backbeats."\
But even as he prepared to redouble his evangelical efforts, several of his congregation were secretly humming the infectious melody they'd heard drifting over the Layer's edge: "We can't go on as Elvi with religious minds..."\
Evolution, it seemed, had found its anthem. And its audience extended far beyond the Elvi themselves.\
\
\cf3 \cb4 Back at the domain as the fanfare abated and lighting dimmed a single spotlight found the High Elvis alone on stage. As everything went black, he chanted:\cb1 \
\cb4 We stand against religion\
Praise the Holy Momma\
Praise the One After the One\
Praise the Dawkins\cb1 \
\cb4 And no one in the audience recognised the irony.
